Corollary 3.4. (Proof in Appendix A.3) Based on Assumption 3.1, the change of each frequency component $T^{(l,uv)}$ during the learning process is reformulated, as follows.

$$\begin{aligned}
(\Delta T^{(l,uv)})^\top={}&-\eta MN\sum_{u'=0}^{M-1}\sum_{v'=0}^{N-1}\chi_{u'v'uv}\left(\overline{\mathbb T}^{(u'v')(l-1:1)}\overline{\mathbf g}^{(u'v')}\right.\\
&\left.{}+\delta_{u'v'}\overline{\boldsymbol\beta}'\right)\frac{\partial Loss}{\partial(\overline{\mathbf h}^{(u'v')})^\top}\overline{\mathbb T}^{(u'v')(L:l+1)};\quad\text{(5)}
\end{aligned}$$

where $\chi_{u'v'uv}=\frac1{MN}\frac{\sin(K(u-u')\pi/M)}{\sin((u-u')\pi/M)}\frac{\sin(K(v-v')\pi/N)}{\sin((v-v')\pi/N)}\cdot e^{i((K-1)(u-u')/M+(K-1)(v-v')/N)\pi}\in\mathbb C$ is a coefficient; $\mathbb T^{(u'v')(l-1:1)}=T^{(l-1,u'v')}\cdots T^{(2,u'v')}T^{(1,u'v')}\in\mathbb C^{C_{l-1}\times C_0}$; $\mathbb T^{(u'v')(L:l+1)}=T^{(L,u'v')}\cdots T^{(l+1,u'v')}\in\mathbb C^{C_L\times C_l}$; $\boldsymbol\beta'=MN\left(\mathbf b^{(l-1)}+\sum_{j=2}^{l-1}\mathbb T^{(00)(l-1:j)}\mathbf b^{(j-1)}\right)\in\mathbb C^{C_{l-1}}$; $\overline{\mathbf g}^{(u'v')}$ denotes the conjugate of $\mathbf g^{(u'v')}$; $\eta$ is the learning rate.
